From 3dd3ef9b1e57e19df13ce1321dc4d4feed1a78b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knfsd-fuzz <knfsd-fuzz@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] sys/linux, executor: add NLM lane calls

Give Stage 1 programs the lane lockd of the nlm-knfsd fixture, where the
knfsd NFSv3 mounts lock through the kernel lockd and lockd listens on TCP
port 4045 in every lane namespace.

- syz_open_nlm_lane_file opens a regular file strictly beneath a lane
  directory (openat2 RESOLVE_BENEATH|NO_XDEV|NO_SYMLINKS|NO_MAGICLINKS,
  NFS superblock), so kernel client byte-range locks on it (fcntl$lock)
  become NLM calls.
- syz_nlm_lane_fh reads the knfsd file handle of such a file through the
  NFS client, checks every constant word of the lane export handle and
  returns the two variable words as nlm_lane_fh_id.
- syz_socket_connect_nlm$client0/1 connects from a lane client namespace
  to the server lockd; syz_send_nlm_rpc sends one record-marked NLM call
  (program 100021, versions 1, 3 and 4, procedures 0-15 and 20-23) whose
  @lane file handle takes nlm_lane_fh_id.  recvfrom$inet_nlm_tcp reads the
  reply.
